\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces The cortico-thalamo-cortical loop and the canonical cortical column, traced as a numbered flow. (1) Sensory input reaches a first-order relay, which (2) drives layer 4. (3–4) The column processes it through the canonical intracolumnar flow (L4 → L2/3 → L5); layer 5 also projects to layer 6 (thin, intracortical). (5–6) Layer 5 sends a driving projection to a higher-order relay, which drives layer 4 of the next cortical area (the transthalamic route). In parallel, layer 6 returns a modulatory projection (thin grey) to both relays and the TRN, the TRN gates both relays (blue, bar-ended), and top-down feedback from a higher cortical area reaches the apical tufts of L2/3 and L5 pyramidal cells in layer 1 (dashed). The relays' collateral input to the TRN is omitted for clarity. Connection classes follow Sherman and Guillery [6, 7].}}{11}{figure.1.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:circuit}{{1.1}{11}{The cortico-thalamo-cortical loop and the canonical cortical column, traced as a numbered flow. (1) Sensory input reaches a first-order relay, which (2) drives layer 4. (3–4) The column processes it through the canonical intracolumnar flow (L4 → L2/3 → L5); layer 5 also projects to layer 6 (thin, intracortical). (5–6) Layer 5 sends a driving projection to a higher-order relay, which drives layer 4 of the next cortical area (the transthalamic route). In parallel, layer 6 returns a modulatory projection (thin grey) to both relays and the TRN, the TRN gates both relays (blue, bar-ended), and top-down feedback from a higher cortical area reaches the apical tufts of L2/3 and L5 pyramidal cells in layer 1 (dashed). The relays' collateral input to the TRN is omitted for clarity. Connection classes follow Sherman and Guillery [6, 7]}{figure.1.1}{}}
